<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hasidism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hasidic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hasidism is a Jewish religious movement founded in the eighteenth century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, emphasising mysticism, joyful worship and devotion to charismatic leaders. It arose among the Jewish communities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. It is counted among the lasting cultural legacies of the Commonwealth's large Jewish population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hasidism.jpg" alt="Hasidism" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: he:משתמש:Eli segal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boyan_tish,_Sukkot_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
